IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 450 of 2021
CITATION: (2021) 3 SCC 713
YEAR: 2021

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: N.V. Ramana, Surya Kant, Aniruddha Bose

PARTIES:
Union of India vs K.A. Najeeb

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act, 1967, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 43D(5) UAPA, Article 21

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
uapa bail, statutory restrictions, constitutional court powers, prolonged incarceration

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Held that statutory bars on bail in special penal statutes (UAPA/NDPS) do not curtail the constitutional powers of High Courts and the Supreme Court under Article 21 where trial is protracted.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 43D(5) UAPA, Article 21.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

